                                      The Scottish Store.


Hecate, the goddess of witchcraft that manipulates Macbeth and others.
Macbeth, a thane in search of power.
Banquo, an ally of Macbeth, who manages his money.

Donalbain, a prince who is worth five cents.
Malcolm, a prince who is worth ten cents.
Duncan, a king who is worth twenty-five cents.
Macduff, the Thane of Fife, who is worth one dollar or one hundred cents.
Lady Macduff, who is worth five dollars or five hundred cents.
Lady Macbeth, who is worth ten dollars or ten hundred cents.

Tybalt, a nobleman in search of a Tab.
Othello, a general in search of a New Line.
Shallow, a justice of the peace who needs some Space; other characters seem grand in comparison to him.
Beatrice, a noblewoman known to often Exclaim!
Ajax, a warrior who is often "quoted".
Shylock, a merchant who likes coin$.
Andromache, a princess who is less 'quoted' than Ajax.
Montague, a nobleman who (Opens a feud.
Capulet, a nobleman who Closes) a feud.
Cordelia, a queen who, often, pauses.
Mark Antony, a politician who - dashes - around.
Puck, a fairy who likes to stop the action periodically.
Cleopatra, a queen who loves to list: things.
Prospero, a ?mysterious? sorcerer.

Arthur, the king of Amsterdam.
Benedick, a nobleman from Bath.
Cicero, a politician from Chester.
Desdemona, a noblewoman from Denmark.
Emilia, a maidservant from Etruria.
Falstaff, a foolish nobleman from France.
Goneril, a princess from Germany.
Helen, the queen of the Highlands.
Isabella, a nun from Italy.
Juliet, a noblewoman from Jersey.
Cressida, a woman from Kent.
Lennox, the thane of Loch Lomond.
Mercutio, a nobleman from Mercia.
Agamemnon, a warrior from Normandy.
Ophelia, a noblewoman from Oxford.
Polonius, a counselor to the ruler of Pisa.
Claudio, a nobleman from Quebec.
Romeo, a nobleman from Rome.
Sebastian, a man from Shrewsbury.
Titania, a fairy queen of Tasmania.
Ulysses, a warrior from Umbria.
Venus, a goddess from Venice.
Viola, a woman from Wales.
Egeus, a nobleman from planet X.
Imogen, a silent noblewoman from York.
Solinus, the duke of Zurich.

Escalus, who knows how much Eye of Newt costs.
The Apothecary, who knows how much Toe of Frog costs.
Valentine, who knows how much Wool of Bat costs.
Timon, who knows how much Tongue of Dog costs.
Adonis, who knows how much Adder's Fork costs.
Bianca, who knows how much Blindworm's Sting costs.
Leonato, who knows how much Lizard's Leg costs.
Hamlet, who knows how much Howlet's Wing costs.

Antonio, who collects $20 bills.
Balthazar, who collects $10 bills.
Christopher Sly, who collects $5 bills.
Dogberry, who collects $1 bills.
Friar Laurence, who collects Quarters.
Gertrude, who collects Dimes.
Horatio, who collects Nickels.
John of Gaunt, who collects Pennies.

The Duke of Milan, who remembers the total cost of the order.
The Duke of Venice, who remembers how much has been paid.

                          Act I: Hecate Prepares To Open The Store.

                              Scene I: Hecate invents money.

[Enter Hecate and Donalbain]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of a stone wall and a good honest son.
[Exit Donalbain]

[Enter Malcolm]
Hecate: Thou art twice Donalbain.
[Exit Malcolm]

[Enter Duncan]
Hecate: Thou art the square of Donalbain.
[Exit Duncan]

[Enter Macduff]
Hecate: Thou art the square of Malcolm.
[Exit Macduff]

[Enter Lady Macduff]
Hecate: Thou art the product of Macduff and Donalbain.
[Exit Lady Macduff]

[Enter Lady Macbeth]
Hecate: Thou art twice Lady Macduff.
[Exit Lady Macbeth]

             Scene II: Hecate declares her opinions on all the interesting secondary personages.

[Enter Tybalt]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of the morning and a beautiful golden embroidered purse.
[Exit Tybalt]

[Enter Othello]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Tybalt and his brother.
[Exit Othello]

[Enter Shallow]
Hecate: Thou art a prompt honest trustworthy healthy noble fellow.
[Exit Shallow]

[Enter Beatrice]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Shallow and Heaven.
[Exit Beatrice]

[Enter Ajax]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Shallow and his bottomless horse.
[Exit Ajax]

[Enter Shylock]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Shallow and a normal smooth road.
[Exit Shylock]

[Enter Andromache]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Beatrice and twice the sum of a little rose and happiness.
[Exit Andromache]

[Enter Montague]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Shylock and a mighty golden town.
[Exit Montague]

[Enter Capulet]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Montague and mistletoe.
[Exit Capulet]

[Enter Cordelia]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Ajax and Othello.
[Exit Cordelia]

[Enter Mark Antony]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Cordelia and a hamster.
[Exit Mark Antony]

[Enter Puck]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Mark Antony and my lamp.
[Exit Puck]

[Enter Cleopatra]
Hecate: Thou art the difference between the cube of a pretty white flower and twice the sum of a horse and a little pony.
[Exit Cleopatra]

[Enter Prospero]
Hecate: Thou art the difference between twice Shallow and a cat.
[Exit Prospero]

             Scene III: Hecate declares her opinions on all the more ordinary secondary personages.

[Enter Arthur]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Macduff and a hairy devil. Thou art the sum of thyself and a lie.
[Exit Arthur]

[Enter Benedick]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Arthur and your mother.
[Exit Benedick]

[Enter Cicero]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Arthur and a handsome hero.
[Exit Cicero]

[Enter Desdemona]
Hecate: Thou art as bold as Macduff.
[Exit Desdemona]

[Enter Emilia]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and the wind.
[Exit Emilia]

[Enter Falstaff]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and an old squirrel.
[Exit Falstaff]

[Enter Goneril]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Falstaff and a plum.
[Exit Goneril]

[Enter Helen]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and warm delicious happiness.
[Exit Helen]

[Enter Isabella]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Helen and the morning.
[Exit Isabella]

[Enter Juliet]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Isabella and her fellow.
[Exit Juliet]

[Enter Cressida]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Juliet and my cat.
[Exit Cressida]

[Enter Lennox]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and the cube of the white moon.
[Exit Lennox]

[Enter Mercutio]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Lennox and the sky.
[Exit Mercutio]

[Enter Agamemnon]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and Othello.
[Exit Agamemnon]

[Enter Ophelia]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Agamemnon and a flower.
[Exit Ophelia]

[Enter Polonius]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Ophelia and a stone wall.
[Exit Polonius]

[Enter Claudio]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Ophelia and an old face.
[Exit Claudio]

[Enter Romeo]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Claudio and my nose.
[Exit Romeo]

[Enter Sebastian]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Claudio and a normal road.
[Exit Sebastian]

[Enter Titania]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and a little old blue furry thing.
[Exit Titania]

[Enter Ulysses]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Titania and the moon.
[Exit Ulysses]

[Enter Venus]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Desdemona and twice Tybalt.
[Exit Venus]

[Enter Viola]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Venus and her mistletoe.
[Exit Viola]

[Enter Egeus]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Venus and cute nose.
[Exit Egeus]

[Enter Imogen]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Egeus and your niece.
[Exit Imogen]

[Enter Solinus]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Imogen and her niece.
[Exit Solinus]

                      Scene IV: Hecate has low opinions of those who manage money.

[Enter Banquo]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Banquo]

[Enter Antonio]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Antonio]

[Enter Balthazar]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Balthazar]

[Enter Christopher Sly]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Christopher Sly]

[Enter Dogberry]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Dogberry]

[Enter Friar Laurence]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Friar Laurence]

[Enter Gertrude]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Gertrude]

[Enter Horatio]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit Horatio]

[Enter John of Gaunt]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit John of Gaunt]

[Enter The Duke of Milan]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit The Duke of Milan]

[Enter The Duke of Venice]
Hecate: Thou art nothing.
[Exit The Duke of Venice]

                              Scene V: Hecate sets the prices of her wares.

[Enter Escalus]
Hecate: Thou art twice Shylock. Thou art the product of yourself and Macduff.
        Thou art the sum of yourself and Duncan. Thou art the sum of yourself and Donalbain.
        Thou art the sum of thyself and a infected wolf.
[Exit Escalus]

[Enter The Apothecary]
Hecate: Thou art the product of the sum of a fair face and hair and the sum of Lady Macduff and Duncan.
        Thou art the sum of thyself and a miserable stinking rotten plague.
        Thou art the sum of thyself and a fatherless goat. Thou art the sum of thyself and Microsoft.
[Exit The Apothecary]

[Enter Valentine]
Hecate: Thou art the sum of Lady Macbeth and twice Duncan. Thou art the difference between thyself and Malcolm.
[Exit Valentine]

[Enter Timon]
Hecate: Thou art an old hairy lying flirt-gill. Thou art the product of thyself and Macduff.
        Thou art the product of thyself and the devil. Thou art the sum of thyself and Duncan.
        Thou art the sum of thyself and a fat pig.
[Exit Timon]

[Enter Adonis]
Hecate: Thou art twice the sum of a brave hero and a rich peaceful kingdom. Thou art the product of thyself and Macduff.
        Thou art the difference between thyself and twice Malcolm.
[Exit Adonis]

[Enter Bianca]
Hecate: Thou art a beautiful sunny warm summer's day. Thou art the sum of thyself and a coward.
        Thou art the product of thyself and Macduff. Thou art the sum of thyself and a hound.
[Exit Bianca]

[Enter Leonato]
Hecate: Thou art an old rural proud trustworthy mighty honest healthy handsome stone wall.
        Thou art the sum of thyself and Arthur. Thou art the difference between thyself and Donalbain.
[Exit Leonato]

[Enter Hamlet]
Hecate: Thou art a good rich cunning trustworthy king. Thou art the product of thyself and Macduff.
        Thou art the sum of thyself and a good rich cunning trustworthy king.
[Exit Hamlet]

                                  Act II: The Store Is Finally Opened.

